The attached PDF consists of two scientific articles by Fangjia Lu et al. and Harm HogenEsch et al., published in 2015 (Journal of Controlled Release) and 2017 (npj Vaccines). Both papers investigate Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from sweet corn, as a vaccine adjuvant. The 2015 paper characterizes Nano-11's physical properties (70-80 nm, positive charge), low cytotoxicity, and its ability to enhance antigen uptake by dendritic cells (DCs) in vitro, activate DCs, and boost antibody responses in mice. The 2017 paper further details Nano-11's in vivo kinetics, showing its comparable or superior adjuvanticity to aluminum hydroxide, transient inflammatory response at the injection site (macrophage-dominated), and efficient targeting of antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes. Both papers conclude Nano-11 is a promising, safe, and cost-effective adjuvant candidate.

The image displays flow cytometry data and corresponding bar graphs from an experiment. The flow plots (labeled "DAY 1" and "DAY 2") compare the uptake of ovalbumin (OVA) by CD11c+ and MHCII+ cells in draining lymph nodes under three conditions: OVA alone, OVA with aluminum hydroxide (AH), and OVA with Nano-11. The bar graphs quantify the mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) of OVA in OVA-positive cells (B) and the percentage of migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) among OVA-positive cells (C) for these conditions and time points. The data visually demonstrate how different adjuvants influence antigen delivery to specific immune cell populations.

